encyclopedia :auneth_lamina do
  name "Auneth Lamina"
  summary "Auneth lamina is porous layered armor shed by Korvathi coastal grazers, cut into wet-backed pads that soften sharp waterborne vibration."
  kind :resource
  subkind :material
  status :complete
  topics :materials, :ecology, :trade, :subject_planetary_life
  prevalence :rare
  appears_when any: { place: [:waterway, :market, :yard] }
  function "Disperses short mechanical impulses through wet fibrous layers and connected pores"
  grades "Irregular shed plates; trimmed pads with sound inner layers; damaged decorative faces"
  descriptive_identity appearance: "Blue-grey plates show pale fibers between thin mineral faces; water beads through fine pores when the edge is pressed.", working: "Wet internal layers flex and spread a sharp impulse across a broader area.", risks: "Dry or mineral-clogged stock becomes brittle; broad force still passes through and can crush the protected object."
  prose <<~PROSE
    The armor of an #{encyclopedia_ref :auneth} combines thin mineral faces with porous fibrous layers. As an outer plate loosens, water enters beneath it and the animal eventually sheds it in irregular pieces. Kept wet and cut to fit a backing, sound inner layers soften sharp mechanical vibration. The material does not stop sustained pressure or make a thin pad proof against a heavy blow.

    Divers along the coast beyond #{ref :oskara} gather shed stock from grazing grounds. The #{ref :ilvaren, "Ilvaren"} teach them to recognize an outer edge ready to part from the living plates below. Broad clean sheets command more money, encouraging some collectors to pry attached armor away. Such sheets arrive with soft tissue on the underside and leave a vulnerable wound on the animal.

    Makers use small pads beneath sensitive submerged instruments and along the rims of animal-care #{encyclopedia_ref :adrel, "adrels"}. At #{ref :house_tereval}, polished outer faces also appear in jewelry and illuminated wall pieces. Decorative dry stock has often lost the flexible layers a diver needs. A purchaser can wet a cut edge and flex it gently to see whether the pale fibers still separate the faces. Repeated drying and mineral-heavy water gradually turn a useful pad into a brittle keepsake.
  PROSE
  cue "Pressure on a wet cut edge squeezes beads of water between blue-grey faces and pale fibers."
  cue "A dealer's immaculate broad plate still has soft tissue clinging beneath it."
  affordance "Sound wet-backed lamina cushions a sensitive submerged fitting or a chamber rim against sharp vibration."
  variation "Naturally shed fragments have irregular edges but can yield several small working pads."
  variation "Polished dry plates make attractive ornaments whose remaining fibers may no longer tolerate a working load."
end
